              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "16938850"
            }
          ],
          "value": "Effector proteins function to alter host cell physiology and promote bacterial survival in host tissues. Involved in the reorganization of late endosome/lysosome (LE/Lys) compartments in mammalian cells. Necessary and sufficient to link kinesin-1 onto the Salmonella-containing vacuole (SCV) membrane. Required for centrifugal extension of lysosomal glycoprotein-rich membrane tubules, known as Salmonella-induced filaments (Sifs), away from the SCV and toward the cell periphery. Required for virulence, but not for intracellular survival and replication in phagocytic cells"
        }
      ],
      "commentType": "FUNCTION"
    },
    {
      "texts": [
        {
          "evidences": [
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "16938850"
            }
          ],
          "value": "Interacts with the host kinesin light chain (KLC), a subunit of the kinesin-1 motor complex"
        }
      ],
      "commentType": "SUBUNIT"
    },
    {
      "commentType": "INTERACTION",
      "interactions": [
        {
          "interactantOne": {
            "uniProtKBAccession": "Q8ZMM8",
            "intActId": "EBI-15598815"
          },
          "interactantTwo": {
            "uniProtKBAccession": "Q07866",
            "geneName": "KLC1",
            "intActId": "EBI-721019"
          },
          "numberOfExperiments": 4,
          "organismDiffer": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "commentType": "SUBCELLULAR LOCATION",
      "note": {
        "texts": [
          {
            "value": "Secreted via the type III secretion system 2 (SPI-2 T3SS), and delivered into the host cell. In phagocytic cells localizes to the Salmonella-containing vacuole (SCV) and tubular extensions from the SCV, Salmonella-induced filaments (Sifs). In epithelial cells localizes to peripheral vesicles, in addition to SCVs and Sifs. Concentrates in detergent-resistant microdomains (DRMs), present on the intracellular membranes of SCVs and Sifs"
          }
        ]
      },
      "subcellularLocations": [
        {
          "location": {
            "value": "Secreted",
            "id": "SL-0243"
          }
        },
        {
          "location": {
            "value": "Host membrane",
            "id": "SL-0380"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "texts": [
        {
          "evidences": [
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "12864852"
            },
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "15813739"
            },
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "19858298"
            }
          ],
          "value": "Expression of the gene is regulated by the two-component regulatory system SsrA/SsrB (PubMed:12864852). It is also activated by SlyA and controlled by the two-component regulatory system PhoP/PhoQ (PubMed:15813739). Expressed in host macrophages and when grown in an acidic environment (PubMed:19858298)"
        }
      ],
      "commentType": "INDUCTION"
    },
    {
      "texts": [
        {
          "evidences": [
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "12864852"
            }
          ],
          "value": "Contains various tandem pentapeptide repeats in the C-terminal region. The pentapeptide motif is required to efficiently recruit kinesin-1. No position is completely conserved in these repeats, whose consensus sequence is A-[DN]-[FLM]-X-X. The C-terminal 38 amino acid residues, specifically, the C-terminal motif LFNEF, are required for peripheral localization of PipB2 and redistribution of lysosomal-associated membrane protein (LAMP). The N-terminal 225 amino acid residues are sufficient for type III translocation and association with Sifs and SCVs, but not accumulation in peripheral vesicles"
        }
      ],
      "commentType": "DOMAIN"
    }
  ],
